## Methods
Materials. Starting materials were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich or TCI and were used without further purification unless otherwise noted.
Tris(dibenzylideneacetone)dipalladium(0), $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}(\mathrm{db}\mathrm{a})_{3}$ , was purchased from Oakwood Products (Fluorochem). Hexane, diethyl ether, ethyl acetate, toluene, acetonitrile and silica gel were purchased from VWR. THF, toluene and acetonitrile were collected from an alumina column solvent purification system. Tetraethylammonium tetrafluoroborate $\mathrm{(TEABF_{4})}^{\cdot}$ ) was recrystallized three times from MeOH, and dried under vacuum at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ . 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaaminotriphenylene hexahydrochloride (HATP 6HCl) was prepared according to a procedure described elsewhere30.
Typical synthesis of $\mathbf{Ni}_{3}(\mathbf{HITP})_{2}$ . A solution of $323\mathrm{mg}\left(1.36\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ of nickel chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ in $20\mathrm{ml}$ of water was added to a solution of $487\mathrm{mg}$ (0.91 mmol) of HATP·6HCl in $140\mathrm{ml}$ of water. Then $4.5\mathrm{ml}$ of concentrated aqueous ammonia $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}O H}$ , $14\mathrm{moll}^{-1}$ ) was added to the mixture. The mixture was stirred in a flask under air bubbling for $^\mathrm{1h}$ at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , then air bubbling was turned off and the mixture was stirred for an additional $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The resulting black powder was isolated by centrifugation, then washed with water (2 times, $\mathrm{180ml}$ ) and ethanol (2 times, $\boldsymbol{180}\mathrm{ml}$ ), dried under a flow of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and stored in a $\Nu_{2}$ -filled glove box until use.
Electrical conductivity. The electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was measured by the four-point van der Pauw method on a pellet with $7\mathrm{mm}$ diameter pressed at 7.8 ton-force $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . A Keithley 2450 source-meter unit was used as a current source and a Keithley 2182A unit was used as a voltmeter.
Standard three-electrode cell assembly. $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (powder) was compressed by hand into a Pt-mesh working electrode (mass loading $\sim5\mathrm{mgcm}^{-2}$ ). Coiled Pt-wire was used as a counter electrode and a AgCl-coated $\mathrm{Ag}$ wire as a pseudo-reference electrode. All measurements were performed under dry and oxygen-free conditions in a $\Nu_{2}$ -filled glove box. A solution of 1 M TEABF4/ACN was used as an electrolyte. Under these conditions, the ferrocene–ferricinium couple $(\mathrm{Fc/Fc^{+}})$ redox couple was measured at $0.43\pm0.02\mathrm{V}$ versus the $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ -wire. All potentials discussed for the three-electrode cell are referenced to $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ .
Electrochemical characterization and analysis. All electrochemical measurements were carried out using a Biologic SP-200 potentiostat. EIS measurements were performed using a multi-sinusoidal signal with an amplitude of $10\mathrm{mV}$ with drift correction as implemented in the Biologic potentiostat.
The equivalent series resistance (ESR) was calculated from the voltage drop $(V_{\mathrm{drop}})$ at the beginning of the discharge curve according to $\mathrm{ESR}(\Omega)=(V_{\mathrm{drop}}/2I)$ , where $I=$ constant discharge current.
The specific gravimetric capacitance $(C_{\mathrm{g}})$ was calculated from galvanostatic discharge curves according to $C_{\mathrm{g}}{=}(a I\Delta t/m\Delta V)$ , where $I=$ constant discharge current, $\Delta t=$ full discharge time, $\Delta V=$ discharge voltage excluding the $V_{\mathrm{drop}}$ (that is, $\Delta V=V_{\mathrm{max}}-V_{\mathrm{drop}}-V_{\mathrm{min}})$ . When calculated from CV traces, $C_{\mathrm{g}}$ was obtained according to $\begin{array}{r}{C_{\mathrm{g}}=(\dot{a}/m(\mathrm{d}V/\mathrm{d}t)\Delta t)\int_{t_{0}}^{t_{1}}I\mathrm{d}}\end{array}$ t, where $I=$ discharge current, $(\mathrm{d}V/\mathrm{d}t)=\mathrm{CV}$ scan rate and $t_{0},t_{1}$ , $\Delta t=$ time for the start of discharge $\left(I<0\right)$ , for the end of discharge, and total discharge time, respectively.
In symmetrical cells, the factor $a=4$ and $m$ is the total mass of both $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ electrodes, while in three-electrode cells $a=1$ and $m$ is the mass of the working $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ electrode.
X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS). X-ray absorption measurements at the nickel K-edge were performed at room temperature at the bending magnet station 20-BM of the Advanced Photon Source (APS) at Argonne National Laboratory. The electron storage ring operated at $7\mathrm{GeV}$ with a stored current of $100\mathrm{mA}$ . The excitation energies were selected with a double crystal monochromator (Si-(111)), which was detuned by $15\%$ , and a harmonic rejection mirror was used to reject the high-energy harmonics. The incident and transmitted beam intensities were monitored using ionization chambers with nitrogen gas. The energy calibration at the nickel K-edge was set by calibrating the inflection point of a nickel foil to $8,343\mathrm{eV.}$
Nickel K-edge spectra were acquired in transmission mode from pressed BN pellets. After electrochemical cycling, cells were disassembled in an Ar glovebox and the electrodes were isolated, mixed, and ground with an appropriate amount of BN. Pellets were pressed under Ar, and sealed with Kapton tape. Absorption spectra were normalized using the Autobk algorithm in the IFEFFIT program of the Horae XAFS analysis suite; a linear fit of the pre-edge line was subtracted from the spectrum before normalization of the edge step to unity. A fourth-order knot-spline polynomial was used to fit the post-edge line. Before Fourier transformation, the EXAFS was multiplied by a Hanning window covering the first and last ${\sim}10\%$ of the data range.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed with a photoelectron spectrometer (K-Alpha XPS System, Thermo Scientific) equipped with a hemispherical analyser. The spectra were acquired using a monochromatized aluminium source Al Ka $\prime E=1,486.6\mathrm{eV},$ ) with a beam spot size of $400\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in a standard emission geometry with an analyser energy resolution of 0.1 eV. Surface charge neutralization was performed by using a low-energy flood gun. Spectra were charge referenced to the adventitious C1s, C–C peak at $284.8\mathrm{eV}$
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were recorded with a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer equipped with a Göbel mirror, rotating sample stage, LynxEye detector and Cu Kα ( $\lambda{=}1.5405\mathrm{\AA}$ ) X-ray source in a $\theta/2\theta$ Bragg–Brentano geometry. An anti-scattering incident source slit $(2\mathrm{mm})$ and an exchangeable detector slit ( $\mathrm{{\cdot}}8\mathrm{{mm}})$ were used. The tube voltage and current were $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $40\mathrm{mA}$ , respectively. Knife-edge attachments were used to remove scattering at low angles. Samples for PXRD were prepared by placing electrodes or powders on a zero-background silicon (510) crystal plate.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were recorded using a Zeiss Supra 55VP FEG SEM with an operating voltage of $3\mathrm{keV}.$
Nitrogen adsorption isotherms were measured with a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 Surface Area and Porosity Analyser. Oven-dried sample tubes equipped with TranSeals (Micromeritics) were evacuated and tared. The samples were transferred to the sample tubes, which were then capped with TranSeals. The samples were heated to $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a vacuum of 0.2 mtorr for $24\mathrm{h}$ , at which point the outgassing rate was less than $0.2\mathrm{mtorr}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The evacuated sample tubes were weighed again, and the sample mass was determined by subtracting the mass of the empty tubes. Nitrogen isotherms were measured using liquid nitrogen baths (77 K). Ultrahigh-purity grade $(99.999\%$ purity) nitrogen and helium, oil-free valves, and gas regulators were used for the free space correction and measurement.
